or":"Pranav Rajpurkar, Jian Zhang, Konstantin Lopyrev, and Percy Liang","source_url":"https://rajpurkar.github.io/SQuAD-explorer/","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Adapted from the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Passage sources originate from Wikipedia; original English text retained."},{"code":"squad-572ea842dfa6aa1500f8d272","subject":"영어","unit":"영어 독해","problem_type":"독해형","difficulty":"응용","question":"[Passage: Neptune]\nNeptune's more varied weather when compared to Uranus is due in part to its higher internal heating. Although Neptune lies over 50% further from the Sun than Uranus, and receives only 40% its amount of sunlight, the two planets' surface temperatures are roughly equal. The upper regions of Neptune's troposphere reach a low temperature of 51.8 K (−221.3 °C). At a depth where the atmospheric pressure equals 1 bar (100 kPa), the temperature is 72.00 K (−201.15 °C). Deeper inside the layers of gas, the temperature rises steadily. As with Uranus, the source of this heating is unknown, but the discrepancy is larger: Uranus only radiates 1.1 times as much energy as it receives from the Sun; whereas Neptune radiates about 2.61 times as much energy as it receives from the Sun. Neptune is the farthest planet from the Sun, yet its internal energy is sufficient to drive the fastest planetary winds seen in the Solar System. Depending on the thermal properties of its interior, the heat left over from Neptune's formation may be sufficient to explain its current heat flow, though it is more difficult to simultaneously explain Uranus's lack of internal heat while preserving the apparent similarity between the two planets.\n\n[Question]\nHow much more energy does Neptune radiate than it receives? ","hint":"Find the sentence in the passage that uses the same key idea as the question.","answer":"2.61","explanation":"1 times as much energy as it receives from the Sun; whereas Neptune radiates about 2.61 times as much energy as it receives from the Sun.","tags":["영어 독해","reading comprehension","Neptune","SQuAD","CC BY-SA 4.0"],"source_name":"Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD)","source_author":"Pranav Rajpurkar, Jian Zhang, Konstantin Lopyrev, and Percy Liang","source_url":"https://rajpurkar.github.io/SQuAD-explorer/","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Adapted from the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Passage sources originate from Wikipedia; original English text retained."},{"code":"squad-572ea842dfa6aa1500f8d273","subject":"영어","unit":"영어 독해","problem_type":"독해형","difficulty":"응용","question":"[Passage: Neptune]\nNeptune's more varied weather when compared to Uranus is due in part to its higher internal heating. Although Neptune lies over 50% further from the Sun than Uranus, and receives only 40% its amount of sunlight, the two planets' surface temperatures are roughly equal. The upper regions of Neptune's troposphere reach a low temperature of 51.8 K (−221.3 °C). At a depth where the atmospheric pressure equals 1 bar (100 kPa), the temperature is 72.00 K (−201.15 °C). Deeper inside the layers of gas, the temperature rises steadily. As with Uranus, the source of this heating is unknown, but the discrepancy is larger: Uranus only radiates 1.1 times as much energy as it receives from the Sun; whereas Neptune radiates about 2.61 times as much energy as it receives from the Sun. Neptune is the farthest planet from the Sun, yet its internal energy is sufficient to drive the fastest planetary winds seen in the Solar System. Depending on the thermal properties of its interior, the heat left over from Neptune's formation may be sufficient to explain its current heat flow, though it is more difficult to simultaneously explain Uranus's lack of internal heat while preserving the apparent similarity between the two planets.\n\n[Question]\nThe current heat flow on Neptune might be explained by what?","hint":"Find the sentence in the passage that uses the same key idea as the question.","answer":"heat left over from Neptune's formation","explanation":"Depending on the thermal properties of its interior, the heat left over from Neptune's formation may be sufficient to explain its current heat flow, though it is more difficult to simultaneously explain Uranus's lack of internal heat while preserving the apparent similarity between the two planets.","tags":["영어 독해","reading comprehension","Neptune","SQuAD","CC BY-SA 4.0"],"source_name":"Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD)","source_author":"Pranav Rajpurkar, Jian Zhang, Konstantin Lopyrev, and Percy Liang","source_url":"https://rajpurkar.github.io/SQuAD-explorer/","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Adapted from the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Passage sources originate from Wikipedia; original English text retained."},{"code":"squad-572ea93ec246551400ce448a","subject":"영어","unit":"영어 독해","problem_type":"독해형","difficulty":"보통","question":"[Passage: Neptune]\nOn 11 July 2011, Neptune completed its first full barycentric orbit since its discovery in 1846, although it did not appear at its exact discovery position in the sky, because Earth was in a different location in its 365.26-day orbit. Because of the motion of the Sun in relation to the barycentre of the Solar System, on 11 July Neptune was also not at its exact discovery position in relation to the Sun; if the more common heliocentric coordinate system is used, the discovery longitude was reached on 12 July 2011.\n\n[Question]\nWhen did Neptune complete it's first barycentric orbit since it's discovery?","hint":"Find the sentence in the passage that uses the same key idea as the question.","answer":"11 July 2011","explanation":"On 11 July 2011, Neptune completed its first full barycentric orbit since its discovery in 1846, although it did not appear at its exact discovery position in the sky, because Earth was in a different location in its 365.","tags":["영어 독해","reading comprehension","Neptune","SQuAD","CC BY-SA 4.0"],"source_name":"Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD)","source_author":"Pranav Rajpurkar, Jian Zhang, Konstantin Lopyrev, and Percy Liang","source_url":"https://rajpurkar.github.io/SQuAD-explorer/","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Adapted from the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Passage sources originate from Wikipedia; original English text retained."},{"code":"squad-572ea93ec246551400ce448b","subject":"영어","unit":"영어 독해","problem_type":"독해형","difficulty":"보통","question":"[Passage: Neptune]\nOn 11 July 2011, Neptune completed its first full barycentric orbit since its discovery in 1846, although it did not appear at its exact discovery position in the sky, because Earth was in a different location in its 365.26-day orbit. Because of the motion of the Sun in relation to the barycentre of the Solar System, on 11 July Neptune was also not at its exact discovery position in relation to the Sun; if the more common heliocentric coordinate system is used, the discovery longitude was reached on 12 July 2011.\n\n[Question]\nWhat is the Earth's orbit?","hint":"Find the sentence in the passage that uses the same key idea as the question.","answer":"365.26-day","explanation":"On 11 July 2011, Neptune completed its first full barycentric orbit since its discovery in 1846, although it did not appear at its exact discovery position in the sky, because Earth was in a different location in its 365.26-day orbit.","tags":["영어 독해","reading comprehension","Neptune","SQuAD","CC BY-SA 4.0"],"source_name":"Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD)","source_author":"Pranav Rajpurkar, Jian Zhang, Konstantin Lopyrev, and Percy Liang","source_url":"https://rajpurkar.github.io/SQuAD-explorer/","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","attribution":"Adapted from the Stanford Question Answering Dataset (SQuAD), licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0. Passage sources originate from Wikiped